<div class="exercise-info">
 <h1>Exercise Name: Leg Press (Standard / Quad-Focused)</h1>
 <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>
This 3D animation demonstrates a standard Leg Press, specifically highlighting the quadriceps (front thigh muscles) in bright color to show muscle engagement. The video shows a figure using a shoulder-width foot placement positioned mid-to-low on the platform to maximize quad activation throughout the press.
 Exercise Name: Leg Press (Standard / Quad-Focused)
 Target Muscles: Quadriceps (rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, vastus intermedius)
 Secondary Muscles: Glutes, Hamstrings

</div></div>
<pre>
<h1>

How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1><u><b><h4>1. Setup</h4></b></u>: Sit on the machine with your back and head supported flush against the backrest. Place your feet flat on the footplate at shoulder-width apart, positioned in the middle section of the plate.
 <u><b><h4>2. Lowering (Eccentric)</h4></b></u>: Release the safety handles. Inhale and slowly lower the platform by bending your knees until your legs form a 90-degree angle. Ensure your hips and lower back do not lift off the seat.
 <u><b><h4>3. Pressing (Concentric)</h4></b></u>: Exhale and push firmly through your mid-foot and heels to drive the weight upward. Stop just before your knees fully lock out at the top to keep constant tension on the quads and protect your joints.


<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

<b>◉Quad Isolation:</b> Provides focused strength and muscle growth for the front of the thighs without requiring balance balance or core stabilization.
 <b>◉Spinal Safety: </b>Relieves vertical spine compression compared to heavy free-weight barbell squats.
 <b>◉Safe Overload: </b>Allows you to safely train with heavier weights near muscle failure using built-in safety catches.



<h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building | 3 – 4 | 8 – 12 | 90 seconds |
| Endurance | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 | 60 seconds |
| Strength | 4 – 5 | 6 – 8 | 2 – 3 minutes |

Control the weight on the way down (2–3 seconds) instead of letting gravity drop the footplate rapidly.

